  <Place Angle="0" AngularSize="0" Constellation="SER2" DataSetType="Sky" Dec="-4.661549900558748" Magnitude="0" Name="Actively forming star system Lynds 483 (NIRCam image)" Opacity="100" RA="18.292104161851608" Rotation="0" Thumbnail="https://data1.wwtassets.org/feeds/jwst/weic2503a/thumb.jpg" ZoomLevel="0.39873413738998986">
    <Description>Shimmering ejections emitted by two actively forming stars make up Lynds 483 (L483). High-resolution near-infrared light captured by the NASA/ESA/CSA James Webb Space Telescope shows incredible new detail and structure within these lobes, including asymmetrical lines that appear to run into one another. L483 is 650 light-years away in the constellation Serpens. [Image description: At the centre is a thin vertical cloud known as Lynds 483 (L483) that is roughly shaped like an hourglass with irregular edges. The lower lobe is slightly cut off. The top lobe is seen in full, petering out at the top.]</Description>
    <ForegroundImageSet>
