  <Place Angle="0" AngularSize="0" Constellation="UMA" DataSetType="Sky" Dec="69.67928204945913" Magnitude="0" Name="M82 (NIRCam image - shorter wavelengths)" Opacity="100" RA="9.931008510996486" Rotation="0" Thumbnail="https://data1.wwtassets.org/feeds/jwst/weic2410c/thumb.jpg" ZoomLevel="0.1286778025996433">
    <Description>M82 hosts a frenzy of star formation, sprouting new stars 10 times faster than the Milky Way galaxy. Webb’s infrared capabilities enabled scientists to peer through curtains of dust and gas that have historically obscured the star formation process. With Webb’s resolution, astronomers can distinguish small, bright compact sources that are either individual stars or star clusters.</Description>
    <ForegroundImageSet>
      <ImageSet BandPass="Visible" BaseDegreesPerTile="0.024078736520664935" BaseTileLevel="0" BottomsUp="False" CenterX="148.96513952168354" CenterY="69.67928261935127" DataSetType="Sky" ElevationModel="False" FileType=".png" Generic="False" Name="M82 (NIRCam image - shorter wavelengths)" OffsetX="2.9392988916827313e-06" OffsetY="2.9392988916827313e-06" Projection="Tan" QuadTreeMap="" Rotation="-52.879999999999676" Sparse="True" StockSet="False" TileLevels="4" Url="https://data1.wwtassets.org/feeds/jwst/weic2410c/L{1}X{2}Y{3}.png" WidthFactor="2" Xcorepipe_ids="jwst|weic2410c" Xcxstatus="in:67c7f65032186919e4cbfcdd">
        <Credits>NASA, ESA, CSA, STScI, A. Bolatto (UMD)</Credits>
        <CreditsUrl>https://esawebb.org/images/weic2410c/</CreditsUrl>
        <Description>M82 hosts a frenzy of star formation, sprouting new stars 10 times faster than the Milky Way galaxy. Webb’s infrared capabilities enabled scientists to peer through curtains of dust and gas that have historically obscured the star formation process. With Webb’s resolution, astronomers can distinguish small, bright compact sources that are either individual stars or star clusters.</Description>
        <ThumbnailUrl>https://data1.wwtassets.org/feeds/jwst/weic2410c/thumb.jpg</ThumbnailUrl>
