  <Place Angle="0" AngularSize="0" Constellation="SEX" DataSetType="Sky" Dec="1.8793081733462924" Magnitude="0" Name="Gravitational lens COSJ100025+015245" Opacity="100" RA="10.007115767905441" Rotation="0" Thumbnail="https://data1.wwtassets.org/feeds/jwst/potm2509h/thumb.jpg" ZoomLevel="0.056698250590784774">
    <Description>Somewhat resembling a ringed planet at first glance, this lens is markedly different from the others presented in the Picture of the Month compilation of gravitational lenses from the COSMOS-Web survey. This shows a rare case in which the lensing galaxy is a thin disc galaxy rather than a giant elliptical galaxy. The dust within the disc absorbs the source’s light, providing a valuable opportunity to study dust in very distant galaxies. This gravitational lens is one of eight featured in the September 2025 Picture of the Month. [Image Description: This image shows a deep galaxy field as seen by Webb, with a distinct gravitational lens at the centre. The lensing galaxy appears in the centre of the frame as a thin disc, with small glowing arcs above and below its centre.]</Description>
    <ForegroundImageSet>
      <ImageSet BandPass="Visible" BaseDegreesPerTile="0.008533822589435225" BaseTileLevel="0" BottomsUp="False" CenterX="150.10673906247482" CenterY="1.8793028571661838" DataSetType="Sky" ElevationModel="False" FileType=".png" Generic="False" Name="Gravitational lens COSJ100025+015245" OffsetX="8.333811122495336e-06" Projection="Tan" QuadTreeMap="" Rotation="19.44000000000003" Sparse="True" StockSet="False" TileLevels="2" Url="https://data1.wwtassets.org/feeds/jwst/potm2509h/L{1}X{2}Y{3}.png" WidthFactor="2">
        <Credits>ESA/Webb, NASA &amp; CSA, G. Gozaliasl, A. Koekemoer, M. Franco</Credits>
        <CreditsUrl>https://esawebb.org/images/potm2509h/</CreditsUrl>
        <ThumbnailUrl>https://data1.wwtassets.org/feeds/jwst/potm2509h/thumb.jpg</ThumbnailUrl>
