  </Place>
  <Place Angle="0" AngularSize="0" Constellation="SEX" DataSetType="Sky" Dec="2.3607549619529986" Magnitude="0" Name="Gravitational lens COSJ100018+022138" Opacity="100" RA="10.005131543918944" Rotation="0" Thumbnail="https://data1.wwtassets.org/feeds/jwst/potm2509c/thumb.jpg" ZoomLevel="0.05675418718756774">
    <Description>Previously confirmed using data from the NASA/ESA Hubble Space Telescope, this lens is revealed in greater detail by Webb. The elliptical lensing galaxy is seen as it was when the Universe was about 4 billion years old. The blue galaxy whose light has been warped into a circle goes back even further, shown as it was when the Universe was less than 2 billion years old. This gravitational lens is one of eight featured in the September 2025 Picture of the Month. [Image Description: This image shows a deep galaxy field as seen by Webb, with a distinct gravitational lens at the centre. A central elliptical galaxy in the foreground has a distorted background galaxy surrounding it as a small, bright blue, circular shape.]</Description>
    <ForegroundImageSet>
      <ImageSet BandPass="Visible" BaseDegreesPerTile="0.008542241773241227" BaseTileLevel="0" BottomsUp="False" CenterX="150.07697565940526" CenterY="2.360749618521391" DataSetType="Sky" ElevationModel="False" FileType=".png" Generic="False" Name="Gravitational lens COSJ100018+022138" OffsetX="8.342032981680886e-06" Projection="Tan" QuadTreeMap="" Rotation="19.939999999999987" Sparse="True" StockSet="False" TileLevels="2" Url="https://data1.wwtassets.org/feeds/jwst/potm2509c/L{1}X{2}Y{3}.png" WidthFactor="2">
