  <Place Angle="0" AngularSize="0" Constellation="SEX" DataSetType="Sky" Dec="2.297117943503319" Magnitude="0" Name="Gravitational lens COSJ100024+021749" Opacity="100" RA="10.006677169006588" Rotation="0" Thumbnail="https://data1.wwtassets.org/feeds/jwst/potm2509b/thumb.jpg" ZoomLevel="0.056936125367912704">
    <Description>The light from the luminous blue foreground galaxy traveled for about 4 billion years to reach us, while the background galaxy’s light has been on a much longer journey, taking more than 11 billion years to reach us. This newly detected lens will help researchers understand galaxy evolution and the formation of stars in dusty environments. This gravitational lens is one of eight featured in the September 2025 Picture of the Month. [Image Description: This image shows a deep galaxy field as seen by Webb, with a distinct gravitational lens at the centre. A central glowing galaxy in the foreground has a warped galaxy from the background as an orange arc on its right side.]</Description>
    <ForegroundImageSet>
      <ImageSet BandPass="Visible" BaseDegreesPerTile="0.008569625830723257" BaseTileLevel="0" BottomsUp="False" CenterX="150.1001600511112" CenterY="2.2971125864471382" DataSetType="Sky" ElevationModel="False" FileType=".png" Generic="False" Name="Gravitational lens COSJ100024+021749" OffsetX="8.36877522531568e-06" Projection="Tan" QuadTreeMap="" Rotation="19.859999999999914" Sparse="True" StockSet="False" TileLevels="2" Url="https://data1.wwtassets.org/feeds/jwst/potm2509b/L{1}X{2}Y{3}.png" WidthFactor="2">
